!! viz-side.allspeak — the editor's Graph sidebar, as a companion module.
!!
!! The sidebar is the reading aid beside the picture: a tabbed panel down the right-hand edge of the Graph pane, whose first tab shows the doc block that the line under a clicked mark belongs to. It is a module for the same reason the drawing is — the editor needs none of it until somebody opens a run — and it is a *separate* module from the drawing for a reason worth stating: the two are about different things, a picture and a reading, they attach to different elements, and either can grow without the other's file growing with it. Each is given its own box by `viz.json`, so neither lays the other out.
!!
!! What it owns, and what it does not. Everything inside its panel: the tab strip, the body, the heading, the prose and the hint that stands in for them before anything is clicked. **It does not find the block.** Which doc block holds a line is a question about the script's structure, and the editor already answers it for Blocks mode; a second answer here would be a second parser to keep in step. So the editor sends the block — its heading and its prose — and this module is a pane. That is also why nothing is read from disk here: the run, the path, and every block all arrive by message.
!!
!! What crosses. One dict in, and it is one of three kinds, so the message says which: a `run` carries the script's path and whether the pane is still on screen, a `block` carries the line that was clicked, whether any block holds it, and the heading and prose to show if one does, and a `tab` carries which tab the reader asked for — from the editor's popup menu rather than from the strip. Nothing goes back — this module has no gestures and nothing to report — and it never needs to know that a picture exists.
!   -- the module, and the panel it owns --

    script VizSide
!! @hash 5424350d
!! @verified 5424350d
!!!
!! What the module holds from the editor's page, and the state the boundary is kept in.
!!
!! The panel is attached **by id** — `viz.json` names it, the same way the editor names every element it owns — so the module has a handle on the page without the editor creating anything for it. Everything the panel is made of is built inside that one element, so the page names one box for the sidebar and no more.
!   -- the page, by id --
    div SideHost
!   -- the panel's own elements, built once --
    div SideTabs            ! the strip along the top
    div SideTab             ! a pool: one element per tab, created at its index
    div SideBody            ! everything under the strip
    span SideHead           ! what the body is showing, as a line
    div SideProse           ! and the prose itself, in the reader's serif
    div SideCode            ! the section's code, for the tab that shows code instead of prose
    div SideHint            ! what stands in for both before a mark is clicked
    div SideStatus          ! the strip along the foot: what the last clicked mark was
    variable SideBuilt      ! 1 once the elements exist, because a message can arrive twice
!   -- the tabs --
    variable SideTabName    ! array: the label on each tab
    variable SideTabCount   ! how many there are
    variable SideTabSel     ! which is up
    variable SideTabI       ! scratch: index over them
    variable SideTabLabel   ! scratch: one tab's label, for the tooltip and the row
!   -- the run, and the block shown for a click --
    variable SideRun        ! the dict the editor sent, as it arrived
    variable SideKind       ! `run` or `block`
    variable SidePath       ! the script or its recording, named for the reader
    variable SideLine       ! the line the clicked mark named
    variable SideVisit      ! which arrival on that line the clicked mark was
    variable SideStatusText ! scratch: the status line, assembled before it is put up
    variable SideValues     ! what the clicked arrival captured: the names the script asked to watch
    variable SideValueKeys  ! scratch: those names, in the script's own order
    variable SideValueText  ! scratch: them and their values, as one run of text
    variable SideValueI     ! scratch: the walk over them
    variable SideValueName  ! scratch: one of those names
    variable SideFound      ! 1 if a doc block holds that line
    variable SideTitle      ! the block's heading, numbered as Blocks mode numbers it
    variable SideProseText  ! its prose
    variable SideTmp        ! general scratch
!   -- the code tab: the script's lines, and the block's own bounds in them --
    variable SideScript     ! the script the picture was drawn from, as the editor sent it
    variable SideLines      ! that script split into lines, once per run
    variable SideLineCount  ! how many lines it has, so a stale bound cannot read past the end
    variable SideFrom       ! the block's first documented line, from the editor
    variable SideTo         ! and its last
    variable SideRow        ! scratch: the walk over the lines the code is built from
    variable SideAt         ! scratch: one line's index, zero-based for `element`
    variable SideSrc        ! scratch: one line of the script, escaped as the walk goes
    variable SideCodeText   ! scratch: the code body, assembled before it is put up

    attach SideHost to `se-graph-side`
    on message go to SideMessage
    release parent
    stop
!! @hash 77dbfaec
!! @verified 77dbfaec
!!!
!! Building the panel, once, and the tabs as the one pool of elements they are.
!!
!! **An AllSpeak element is a declared symbol, so a tab is a symbol rather than something grown at run time** — the same constraint the drawing's marks live under. The pool is `SideTab`, sized here to the number of tabs and created one element at a time at its own index, which is the shape Blocks mode's table of contents already uses; **so a tab is a name in `SideTabName`, a branch in `SideRender`, and whatever that branch shows** — the next one after the code tab was those three things and nothing else, which is the shape to keep.
!!
!! The strip is built before the body so that it sits above it, and the three body elements are created once and emptied rather than replaced: a panel that rebuilt its own children on every click would flicker, and a hidden panel that keeps them is the cheaper thing to show again.
!!
!! Appendix
!!
!! §1 **The body holds no padding of its own, because the two text components carry their own margins.** That is Graham's arrangement and it is the better one: the synopsis and the body are two things a reader reads in two different ways, and giving each its own inset is what keeps them from being pinned to the panel's edges at one width and lost in it at another. A single padding on the body would look identical today and would have to be unpicked the moment one of them wanted a wider gutter than the other.
!!
!! §2 **The prose is the same reading as Blocks gives it** — Georgia on the pane's own paper — because a doc block a reader has met in Blocks should not meet them differently here. The colour is set rather than inherited: the panel is light now, but a component that relied on that would break the day the panel's colour changed, and light-grey-on-light is the failure nobody notices until it ships.
!!
!! §3 `pre-wrap` rather than running the text together, because the doc-block convention writes one paragraph to a line and those lines are the paragraphs: re-flowing them here would throw the shape away, which is the one thing the convention is for.
!!
!! §4 **The status bar, along the foot of the panel.** It is the one place a reader can be told something about the *mark* rather than about the block the mark's line belongs to — and along a single row of dots the block is the same for every one of them, so the block is exactly what cannot tell them apart. Created after the body so it sits below it, and given a top border rather than a second background: the panel is one surface, and a reader should see the bar as the panel's own foot, not as a third thing wedged into it.
SideBuild:
    if SideBuilt is 1 return
    put 1 into SideBuilt

    create SideTabs in SideHost
    set the style of SideTabs to `display:flex;gap:2px;padding:4px 8px 0 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;flex-shrink:0`

    create SideBody in SideHost ! §1
    set the style of SideBody to `flex:1;overflow-y:auto;box-sizing:border-box`
    create SideHead in SideBody
    set the style of SideHead to `display:block;font-size:11px;letter-spacing:0.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#8a8a80;margin:14px 16px 6px 16px`
    create SideProse in SideBody
    set the style of SideProse to `font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5;white-space:pre-wrap;color:#222;margin:0 16px 16px 16px` ! §2–§3
    ! **The code is shown in the script's own shape, which is why it is `pre` and not `pre-wrap`.** A
    ! wrapped line of code reads as two statements, and a reader comparing this panel with the picture is
    ! comparing line for line. The horizontal overflow scrolls rather than wraps for the same reason, and
    ! the font is the one the editor uses so the two panes look like the same script.
    create SideCode in SideBody
    set the style of SideCode to `font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;font-size:12px;line-height:1.45;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto;color:#222;margin:0 16px 16px 16px`
    create SideHint in SideBody
    set the style of SideHint to `color:#8a8a80;font-size:12px;font-style:italic;margin:14px 16px`

    create SideStatus in SideHost ! §4
    set the style of SideStatus to `border-top:1px solid #ddd;padding:6px 16px;font-size:12px;color:#666;flex-shrink:0;min-height:18px`

    ! The names first, then the strip built from them, so the strip and this list cannot disagree
    ! about how many tabs there are.
    put 2 into SideTabCount
    set the elements of SideTabName to SideTabCount
    index SideTabName to 0
    put `Doc block` into SideTabName
    index SideTabName to 1
    put `Code` into SideTabName

    put 0 into SideTabSel
    set the elements of SideTab to SideTabCount
    put 0 into SideTabI
    while SideTabI is less than SideTabCount
    begin
        index SideTab to SideTabI
        create SideTab in SideTabs
        index SideTabName to SideTabI
        put SideTabName into SideTabLabel
        set the content of SideTab to SideTabLabel
        set attribute `title` of SideTab to `Show the ` cat SideTabLabel cat ` for the line the pointer is over`
        on click SideTab go to SideTabClick
        add 1 to SideTabI
    end
    gosub to SideTabsDraw
    return
!! @hash 01f12ebf
!! @verified 8ee3c368
!!!
!! The tab strip's one piece of state, which is which tab is up.
!!
!! The selected tab is the only one that differs, and it is redrawn from `SideTabSel` rather than remembered on the element: the same rule the TOC rows follow, so a tab cannot be lit and not be the one being shown.
SideTabsDraw:
    put 0 into SideTabI
    while SideTabI is less than SideTabCount
    begin
        index SideTab to SideTabI
        put `padding:4px 10px;font-size:11px;border-radius:3px 3px 0 0;cursor:pointer;background:` into SideTmp
        if SideTabI is SideTabSel put SideTmp cat `#1e88e5;color:white` into SideTmp
        else put SideTmp cat `#e4e2da;color:#555` into SideTmp
        set the style of SideTab to SideTmp
        add 1 to SideTabI
    end
    return

SideTabClick:
    put the index of SideTab into SideTabSel
    gosub to SideTabShow
    stop
!! @hash c6dc05c2
!! @verified e76c1079
!!!
!! Put the selected tab up, which is one act however it was asked for.
!!
!! **Two ways in and one answer.** A tab can be chosen by clicking the strip or by the editor's popup menu, and both end here: the strip's own handler sets `SideTabSel` and comes here, and so does the menu's message. That is deliberate rather than tidy — a reader who could reach a tab two ways that disagreed would be looking at a panel nobody could predict — and it is why the rendering below takes no argument: the state it draws from was set by whoever asked.
SideTabShow:
    gosub to SideTabsDraw
    gosub to SideRender
    return
!! @hash 09024525
!!!
!! What the body shows, which is one of three things: a block, a line in no block, or nothing yet — and each of them in one of two ways, the doc block's prose or the section's code.
!!
!! **The middle case is a real answer rather than an error.** The lines before the first doc block and the code between two of them are outside the convention by design, and a reader who clicks a mark there should be told that rather than shown the last block somebody looked at — which is what a panel that only ever replaced its contents on a hit would do.
!!
!! **The tab decides which of the two a line is shown as, and the branches below are that decision.** `SideRenderCode` builds the section's code — its lines with the doc-block lines taken out — and `SideCodeBuild` is the rule that does it, which is one rule and the whole of what "the code, minus the doc blocks" means: a block's prose and its tail are the lines that begin with an exclamation mark, and what is left between the block's two documented bounds is the code. Nothing is re-parsed and no second copy of the block model exists here; the editor owns that and it sent the bounds.
!!
!! **The line the pointer named is picked out, when it is one of the lines shown.** Here rather than in the editor because it is a fact about the *rendering*: the same line is highlighted in the code and is the subject of the prose, and a reader switching tabs should see the same line picked out in both. A line outside the section has nothing to pick out, and gets no mark.
!!
!! The hint is what the panel says whenever there is nothing to show, and it is the only thing in this module that tells a reader what the panel is for.
!!
!! Appendix
!!
!! §1 **A line no block holds still has code, and this tab shows it.** The editor sends the *gap's* bounds for such a line — the run of code between the block before the pointer and the block after it — so the slice below is the same one a block gets, and the sentence that is the doc-block tab's whole answer becomes this tab's footnote, under the code. That is Graham's rule for this panel doing its work (`TODO-viz.md`, "Settled decisions"): a reader pointing at the code between two blocks is handed that code rather than sent back to the editor for it. An *empty* gap — two blocks written back to back — shows no code and the sentence, which is the truth about it.
SideRender:
    ! The status bar first, and unconditionally: it is about the mark, not about the block, so it says its
    ! piece whether or not the line turned out to be in one.
    gosub to SideStatusDraw
    ! **Every body element is emptied first, and in one place.** The panel keeps its elements rather than
    ! replacing them, so a branch shows one thing by *saying* that it shows that thing — and the branch
    ! that has to empty the others is the one a reader changes a tab with. Doing it here rather than inside
    ! each branch is what stops a tab from leaving the last tab's text standing under its own.
    set the content of SideHead to ``
    set the content of SideProse to ``
    set the content of SideCode to ``
    set the content of SideHint to ``
    if SideKind is not `block` go to SideRenderHint
    if SideTabSel is 1 go to SideRenderCode
    if SideFound is 0 go to SideRenderOutside
    set the content of SideHead to SideTitle
    set the content of SideProse to SideProseText
    return

SideRenderOutside:
    set the content of SideHead to `Line ` cat SideLine
    set the content of SideProse to ``
    set the content of SideHint to `This line is outside any doc block.`
    return

SideRenderCode:
    if SideFound is 0 set the content of SideHead to `Line ` cat SideLine ! §1
    if SideFound is 1 set the content of SideHead to SideTitle
    gosub to SideCodeBuild
    set the content of SideCode to SideCodeText
    if SideFound is 0 set the content of SideHint to `This line is outside any doc block.`
    return

SideCodeBuild:
    put `` into SideCodeText
    put SideFrom into SideRow
    while SideRow is not greater than SideTo
    begin
        ! A bound from a message that does not match the script this panel holds is not worth a crash:
        ! the code is shown short rather than wrongly.
        if SideRow is greater than SideLineCount go to SideCodeDone
        take 1 from SideRow giving SideAt
        put element SideAt of SideLines into SideSrc
        if left 1 of SideSrc is not `!`
        begin
            ! **Escaped here, because code is text a person reads.** An `&` or a `<` that reached the
            ! element as markup would be eaten and a section full of them would show a script nobody
            ! wrote — the pane escapes the same characters for the same reason. The ampersand goes first,
            ! or it would escape the ampersands the other two have just written.
            replace `&` with `&amp;` in SideSrc
            replace `<` with `&lt;` in SideSrc
            replace `>` with `&gt;` in SideSrc
            if SideRow is SideLine
            begin
                put SideCodeText cat `<span style="background:#dcebfb;border-radius:2px">` cat SideSrc cat `</span>` into SideCodeText
            end
            else put SideCodeText cat SideSrc into SideCodeText
            put SideCodeText cat newline into SideCodeText
        end
        add 1 to SideRow
    end
SideCodeDone:
    return

SideRenderHint:
    set the content of SideHead to ``
    set the content of SideProse to ``
    set the content of SideHint to `Point at a line of the script in the picture to see the doc block it belongs to, or its code on the Code tab.`
    return
!! @hash 55b7306e
!! @verified b04946e6
!!!
!! The status bar: what the script asked to have watched, at the mark it was watching.
!!
!! **It is the values and not the mark, and that is a division made on 2026-10-05.** It used to carry the mark's own figures too — "line 254 visit 23 of 44" — and Graham moved them out: they are read while pointing at a dot, and on a big screen the foot of this panel is the other side of the room from the picture, so the pane says them itself, one line under its own status line, where the eye already is. What is left here is the other half of the same message: the numbers the script asked to have shown by writing `@show`, which belong beside the block because they are about the work the line did rather than about which dot was clicked.
!!
!! **The figures still decide whether the bar is drawn at all.** A press that named no mark leaves an empty bar, and so does a mark whose script asked to watch nothing — the panel is not the place for a line that says only that there is nothing to say.
!!
!! Appendix
!!
!! §1 **And the values, which is what a script's `@show` was for.** This is where the question that made somebody write the attribute is answered: the bar is read at the same moment as the picture, so the number and the run it belongs to arrive together without a second place to look.
!!
!! §2 **In name order**, which is what `the json keys of` gives and the reason to use it here: two visits compared across a run read the same way round, and a reader scanning a column of status lines is comparing them. The script's own order (`@show N, Total`) would put the names in whatever order each line happened to be written in, which is a difference nobody asked to see.
!!
!! §3 **`the json count of` and `element N of`, because these keys are one json list and one value.** A variable is a holder with slots: `index X to N` picks a slot, and a *json list* is a single value occupying a single slot — so `index Keys to 0` with `put Keys into Name` hands back the whole list (correctly), and the elements of that list are reached with `element N of`. `split` is the other way round: it fills a slot per line, which is why this editor's buffer walk uses `index` and `has element` on it. Both are index-based and neither replaces the other; using the wrong one prints a list where a value belongs.
SideStatusDraw:
    if SideVisit is less than 1
    begin
        set the content of SideStatus to ``
        return
    end
    put `` into SideStatusText
    if SideValues is not empty ! §1–§2
    begin
        put the json keys of SideValues into SideValueKeys
        put `` into SideValueText
        put 0 into SideValueI
        while SideValueI is less than the json count of SideValueKeys ! §3
        begin
            put element SideValueI of SideValueKeys into SideValueName
            if SideValueText is not empty put SideValueText cat `  ` into SideValueText
            put SideValueText cat SideValueName cat `=` cat property SideValueName of SideValues into SideValueText
            add 1 to SideValueI
        end
        if SideStatusText is not empty put SideStatusText cat `   ` into SideStatusText
        put SideStatusText cat SideValueText into SideStatusText
    end
    set the content of SideStatus to SideStatusText
    return
!! @hash 1a7e607d
!! @verified 591e92f4
!!!
!! The boundary: one message in, and the module parks until the next one.
!!
!! `release parent` in the plumbing above is what lets the editor's `run` return, and this is what carries on from there: the panel is built on the first message rather than at load, because a module that is loaded and never opened should cost nothing but its attach.
!!
!! The three kinds are told apart by the message's own `kind` rather than by which of its properties are present, so a field can be added to any of them without the others being read wrongly. **The tab switch is answered before `SideKind` is written, and that is a fault rather than a tidy order.** `SideKind` is the panel's *own state* — which of three things it is showing — and not the last message's kind: `SideRender` reads it to choose between a block, a line in no block and its standing hint, so a `tab` message that overwrote it would show the hint over the block the panel is holding. A tab is only ever a different *view* of what is already there, so it must not disturb what it is a view of. **And it is tested before the `open` field of a run is looked at** for the same reason one step on: a `tab` message carries no `open`, and a message whose absent field read as `0` would be taken for the pane being taken off the screen.
!!
!! Appendix
!!
!! §1 **The menu's tab switch, and it is only a selection.** The panel already holds the line it was last told and the block for it, so putting a tab up is the same act a click on the strip is — hence `SideTabShow`, which both go through. Nothing is sent to the pane and nothing is re-read: a tab is a view of the line the sidebar already has, and asking the pane again would answer about whatever the pointer is over *now*, which is the thing the reader may have frozen precisely to get away from.
!!
!! §2 The build is asked for here as well, because this is the one branch that leaves before the shared `gosub to SideBuild` above it — and a message can arrive before anything has built the panel.
SideMessage:
    put the message into SideRun
    put property `kind` of SideRun into SideTmp
    if SideTmp is `tab` go to SideTabArrived
    put SideTmp into SideKind
    gosub to SideBuild
    if SideKind is `block` go to SideBlockArrived
    if property `open` of SideRun is 0 go to SideClosed
    put property `path` of SideRun into SidePath
    ! **The script, split into lines once per run** rather than per click: a line arrives with every
    ! pointer move, and re-splitting a thousand-line script for each of them would be paid by the gesture
    ! that has to stay cheap. `json split` divides on the newline by default, which is the whole reason no
    ! delimiter is named here.
    put property `script` of SideRun into SideScript
    json split SideScript into SideLines
    put the json count of SideLines into SideLineCount
    ! A new run clears whatever the last click left up, because the block it showed belonged to another
    ! script — the picture has been replaced, and so has everything the sidebar was saying about it. The
    ! mark's figures go with it: they counted a line in a run that is no longer on screen.
    put 0 into SideVisit
    put json of `{}` into SideValues
    gosub to SideRender
    stop

SideTabArrived:
    gosub to SideBuild ! §1–§2
    put property `sel` of SideRun into SideTabSel
    gosub to SideTabShow
    stop

SideBlockArrived:
    put property `line` of SideRun into SideLine
    put property `visit` of SideRun into SideVisit
    ! The values the script asked to have watched, if it asked for any. Passed through, not interpreted:
    ! what `@show Total` means was settled by the recorder that read it.
    put property `values` of SideRun into SideValues
    put property `found` of SideRun into SideFound
    put property `title` of SideRun into SideTitle
    put property `prose` of SideRun into SideProseText
    put property `from` of SideRun into SideFrom
    put property `to` of SideRun into SideTo
    ! Said here as well as in the editor, so that the two halves of the relay can be told apart when only
    ! one of them is working: this line means the panel was reached, and its absence means the message
    ! never crossed. It names its own subject so a copied line still makes sense on its own.
    log `sidebar: showing line ` cat SideLine cat ` visit ` cat SideVisit cat ` found=` cat SideFound cat ` title=` cat SideTitle
    gosub to SideRender
    stop

!   The pane is off screen. Nothing is said and nothing is cleared: the panel is hidden with the rest
!   of the Graph area, so it will be showing the same block when the pane comes back, which is what a
!   reader returning to the same run expects.
SideClosed:
    stop
!! @hash 41deff7c
!! @verified cd2f39fb
!!!
